  ⎿  Added 33 lines
      202  
      203  # Examples/Plot Types
      204  
      205 +Every plot type can be called two ways: through `plt` with `'p
          +lot.type' => 'bar'`,                                          
      206 +or through the same-named helper subroutine, `bar( ... )`, whi
          +ch is a thin wrapper                                          
      207 +that fills in `'plot.type'` and calls `plt` for you.  Everythi
          +ng documented for a                                           
      208 +plot type therefore works in either form, and works identicall
          +y whether the plot                                            
      209 +is alone or one panel of a `plots` grid.                      
      210 +                                                              
      211 +## Which helper takes which data?                             
      212 +                                                              
      213 +The fastest way to pick a plot type is to start from the shape
          + of the data you                                              
      214 +already have in Perl:                                         
      215 +                                                              
      216 +| `data` you have | Helpers that take it | Notes |            
      217 +| -------- | ------- | ------- |                              
      218 +|hash of numbers, `A => 1`|`bar`, `barh`, `pie`|one bar/wedge 
          +per key|                                                      
      219 +|hash of array refs, `A => [1,2,3]`|`boxplot`, `violin`, `hist
          +`, `hexbin`, `hist2d`, `scatter`, `venn_proportional_area`|one
          + distribution/series per key; `hexbin`, `hist2d` and `scatter`
          + need exactly 2 (or 3) keys|                                  
      220 +|hash of hashes, `A => { X => 1 }`|`bar`, `barh`, `colored_tab
          +le`|grouped/stacked bars, or a matrix|                        
      221 +|hash of `[ \@x, \@y ]` pairs|`plot`|one labelled line per key
          +|                                                             
      222 +|hash of arrays of `[ \@x, \@y ]` pairs|`wide`|repeated runs o
          +f the same curve, summarised|                                 
      223 +|hash of hashes of array refs|`scatter`|several labelled sets,
          + each with its own x/y (and colour)|                          
      224 +|a single array ref|`hist`, `boxplot`, `violin`|the one-series
          + shorthand|                                                   
      225 +|array of `[ \@x, \@y ]` pairs|`plot`, `wide`|unlabelled lines
          +|                                                             
      226 +|2-D array (array of array refs)|`imshow`|a raster/heatmap; st
          +rings allowed via `stringmap`|                                
      227 +                                                              
      228 +A few conventions hold across all of them:                    
      229 +                                                              
      230 +- Keys are used in **sorted order** unless you pass `key.order
          +` (accepted by                                                
      231 +  every plot type except `pie`, `hist` and `imshow`).         
      232 +- `title`, `xlabel`, `ylabel`, `suptitle`, `set_xlim`, `legend
          +` and the rest of                                             
      233 +  Matplotlib's `ax`/`fig`/`plt` methods are accepted by every 
          +plot type; see                                                
      234 +  [Options](#options).                                        
      235 +- Anything that is not recognised is reported as an error list
          +ing the arguments                                             
      236 +  that *are* accepted, rather than being silently ignored.    
      237 +                                                              
      238  Consider the following helper subroutines to generate data to 
           plot:
      239  
      240      sub linspace { # mostly written by Grok

