      1436  
      1437  Plot 2D array of numbers as an image
      1438  
      1439 +### Entering data                                            
      1440 +                                                             
      1441 +`data` is a 2-D array — an array of array refs — and nothing 
           +else; a hash is an                                           
      1442 +error.  Row `0` is drawn at the top, so `set_ylim` reversed, 
           +or Matplotlib's                                              
      1443 +`invert_yaxis`, is what you want if your first row is meant t
           +o be the bottom:                                             
      1444 +                                                             
      1445 +    my @grid;                                                
      1446 +    foreach my $i (0 .. 360) {                               
      1447 +            foreach my $j (0 .. 360) {                              
      1448 +                    push @{ $grid[$i] }, sin($i * $pi/180) * cos($j 
           +);                                                           
      1449 +            }                                                       
      1450 +    }                                                        
      1451 +    imshow(                                                  
      1452 +            'output.file' => '/tmp/grid.svg',                       
      1453 +            data          => \@grid,                                
      1454 +            cblabel       => 'sin(x) * cos(x)',                     
      1455 +    );                                                       
      1456 +                                                             
      1457 +The cells may hold **strings** instead of numbers, as long as
           + `stringmap` gives                                           
      1458 +the meaning of each one; the strings are then numbered for co
           +loring and the                                               
      1459 +colorbar is replaced by a legend of the mapped names.  This i
           +s what makes                                                 
      1460 +`imshow` usable for categorical rasters — sequence annotation
           +, land cover,                                                
      1461 +state-over-time diagrams — and there is a worked example unde
           +r                                                            
      1462 +[Secondary Structure Prediction (DSSP)](#secondary-structure-
           +prediction-dssp).                                            
      1463 +                                                             
      1464 +Because `imshow` produces a colorbar per subplot, `shared.col
           +orbar` is often                                              
      1465 +worth setting when several panels show the same quantity: it 
           +gives them one                                               
      1466 +colorbar, and hence one color scale, so the panels can be com
           +pared.                                                       
      1467 +                                                             
      1468  ### options
      1469 +                                                             
      1470  | Option | Description | Example |
      1471  | -------- | ------- | ------- 
      1472  |`cblabel`| colorbar label | `cblabel => 'sin(x) * cos(x)',`
      1473  |`cbdrawedges` |draw edges for colorbar | |
      1474  |`cblocation` | 'left', 'right', 'top', 'bottom'| `cblocation
             => 'left',`|
      1475  |`cborientation`|  None, or 'vertical', 'horizontal' | 
      1476 +|`cbpad`| fraction of the original axes between the image and
           + the colorbar; the default 0.05 is often too big for a short,
           + wide image | `cbpad => 0.01,`|                              
      1477  |`cmap`| # The Colormap instance or registered colormap name 
            used to map scalar data to colors.|
      1478 +|`colorbar.on`| draw the colorbar; on by default, `0` turns i
           +t off|`'colorbar.on' => 0`|                                  
      1479 +|`shared.colorbar`| 0-based indices of the subplots that shou
           +ld share one colorbar, and therefore one color scale|`'shared
           +.colorbar' => [0,1]`|                                        
      1480 +|`stringmap`| a hash giving the meaning of each string used i
           +n `data`, which also makes string data legal|`stringmap => { 
           +H => 'Alpha helix' }`|                                       
      1481  |`vmax`| float |
      1482  |`vmin`| float | 
