● Update(README.md)
  ⎿  Added 71 lines
      2147  
      2148  ## scatter
      2149  
      2150 +Plot points from a hash of arrays.  Beyond x and y, a scatter
           +plot can carry a                                             
      2151 +third number per point as **color**, which is where most of `
           +scatter`'s options                                           
      2152 +go.                                                          
      2153 +                                                             
      2154 +### Entering data                                            
      2155 +                                                             
      2156 +`data` takes two shapes, and which one you passed is worked o
           +ut from whether the                                          
      2157 +values are arrays or hashes.                                 
      2158 +                                                             
      2159 +**1. One set (hash of 2 or 3 array refs).** All the arrays mu
           +st be the same                                               
      2160 +length.  Keys are taken in case-insensitive sorted order: the
           + first is x, the                                             
      2161 +second y, and a third — if present — is the value each point 
           +is colored by, which                                         
      2162 +also gets a colorbar.  Exactly 2 or 3 keys are allowed; anyth
           +ing else is an                                               
      2163 +error.  The keys become the axis labels, so naming them for t
           +he quantity they                                             
      2164 +hold pays off:                                               
      2165 +                                                             
      2166 +    scatter(                                                 
      2167 +            'output.file' => '/tmp/scatter.svg',                    
      2168 +            data          => {                                      
      2169 +                    Height => \@height,    # x                      
      2170 +                    Weight => \@weight,    # y                      
      2171 +                    Age    => \@age,       # colour + colorbar      
      2172 +            },                                                      
      2173 +            color_key     => 'Age',    # say so rather than relying 
           +n the sort                                                   
      2174 +            cmap          => 'viridis',                             
      2175 +    );                                                       
      2176 +                                                             
      2177 +Sorted order is convenient but fragile — rename a key and the
           + axes swap.  Use                                             
      2178 +`keys` to fix the roles positionally, or `color_key` to name 
           +the color column                                             
      2179 +explicitly, as above:                                        
      2180 +                                                             
      2181 +            keys => [ 'Weight', 'Height', 'Age' ],    # x, y, colour
      2182 +                                                             
      2183 +**2. Several labelled sets (hash of hashes of array refs).** 
           +The outer key is                                             
      2184 +the set's legend label; each inner hash is a set of 2 or 3 ar
           +rays read exactly as                                         
      2185 +in form 1.  This is the form to use for "the same measurement
           +, split by group":                                           
      2186 +                                                             
      2187 +    scatter(                                                 
      2188 +            'output.file' => '/tmp/by.group.svg',                   
      2189 +            data          => {                                      
      2190 +                    Male   => { Height => \@mh, Weight => \@mw },   
      2191 +                    Female => { Height => \@fh, Weight => \@fw },   
      2192 +            },                                                      
      2193 +            'set.options' => {                                      
      2194 +                    Male   => 'marker = "v", color = "blue"',       
      2195 +                    Female => 'marker = "o", color = "red"',        
      2196 +            },                                                      
      2197 +    );                                                       
      2198 +                                                             
      2199 +With three inner keys, every set is colored by its own third 
           +column against a                                             
      2200 +shared colorbar.  `color_key` then names an **inner** key, an
           +d it must exist in                                           
      2201 +each set — naming a key that is not there is an error rather 
           +than being ignored.                                          
      2202 +                                                             
      2203 +### options                                                  
      2204 +                                                             
      2205 +| Option | Description | Example |                           
      2206 +| -------- | ------- | ------- |                             
      2207 +|`cmap`| the colormap used when a third key colors the points
           +; `gist_rainbow` by default | `cmap => 'viridis'` |          
      2208 +|`color_key`| which key of `data` holds the color values, rat
           +her than letting the sort decide.  For the multi-set form thi
           +s is an inner key, and it must be present in every set | `col
           +or_key => 'Age'` |                                           
      2209 +|`colorbar.on`| draw the colorbar; on by default | `'colorbar
           +.on' => 0` |                                                 
      2210 +|`keys`| array ref fixing the roles of the keys positionally:
           + x, y, then color | `keys => ['Weight', 'Height', 'Age']` |  
      2211 +|`logscale`| an array of the axes to put on a log scale | `lo
           +gscale => ['x', 'y']` |                                      
      2212 +|`set.options`| arguments passed straight to Matplotlib's `ax
           +.scatter`: `marker`, `color`, `alpha`, `s`, …  A **scalar** f
           +or the single-set form; a **hash keyed by set name** for the 
           +multi-set form.  Options for a set that has no data are an er
           +ror | `'set.options' => 'marker = "v", alpha = 0.4'` |       
      2213 +|`shared.colorbar`| 0-based indices of the subplots that shou
           +ld share one colorbar, and hence one color scale | `'shared.c
           +olorbar' => [0,1]` |                                         
      2214 +|`show.legend`| on by default; `0` suppresses the legend of s
           +et labels | `'show.legend' => 0` |                           
      2215 +                                                             
      2216 +`xlabel` and `ylabel` default to the names of the keys used f
           +or x and y; set them                                         
      2217 +explicitly to override.  The colorbar is labelled with the na
           +me of the color key,                                         
      2218 +which `cblabel` overrides, and the other colorbar options fro
           +m                                                            
      2219 +[Color Bars](#color-bars-colorbars) apply as well.           
      2220 +                                                             
      2221  ### single, simple plot
      2222      scatter(
      2223              fh            => $fh,

